  "text": "The experiments examined how changing the temperature affected the speed of head regrowth in two flatworm types that differ markedly in the way their regeneration zones are organized. As anticipated, cooler conditions slowed the process at every body position tested, but the slowdown was especially pronounced when the cut was made farther back from the head region. The two flatworm types also showed distinct patterns of regrowth speed, suggesting differences in the underlying regenerative system. Lowering the temperature appeared to split regeneration into two major steps: the creation of a new growth mass and the later specialization of its cells. The initial growth mass formed fairly well even at temperatures just above freezing, whereas the specialization step was almost halted under the same cold conditions. These findings raise doubts about the idea that regeneration provides an adaptive advantage, since many flatworm species live and reproduce at temperatures far below those needed for successful regrowth."
